  "content": {
    "id": "p10-q37-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "37",
    "prompt": "Let $p$ and $q$ be consecutive prime numbers greater than 2. Prove that $p+q$ is the product of three integers, each greater than 1.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$p+q=2ab$, where $a,b>1$ are integers.\n\nThe integer midpoint of the two odd primes lies strictly between them, so it is composite.",
      "worked": "Without loss of generality, let $p<q$. Both primes are odd, so $m=\\frac{p+q}{2}$ is an integer and $p<m<q$.\n\nIf $m$ were prime, it would be a prime strictly between the consecutive primes $p$ and $q$, a contradiction. Hence $m$ is composite. Write $m=ab$ for integers $a,b>1$. Then\n$$\\begin{align*}p+q&=2m\\\\p+q&=2ab.\\end{align*}$$\nThus $p+q$ is the product of the three integers $2,a,b$, each greater than 1.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 62
  },
